;;; The ladder's second rung taken by an obligation rule a program loads and
;;; does not activate — **W97**, register row `W97/unactivated`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `std.algebra` imported for `total-owed`, which the program never writes,
;;; beside two relations of its own that nothing closes, `at` and `near`,
;;; over one colour and two houses. Every import brings its module whole, so
;;; `std.algebra`'s two obligation rules, `total-owed` and `surjective-owed`,
;;; are loaded; no fact activates either. The control,
;;; `rung-unactivated-no-import.ein`, is this program with the import deleted.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Hypothesis generation is a ladder: the program's hrules, else what would
;;; pay the debts of an obligation activated at root, else the blind
;;; enumerator. Here there is no hrule and no obligation is activated, so H
;;; is the blind enumerator's, eleven candidates of `at` and `near` over the
;;; program's three names, and nothing restricts them: R is the whole cube,
;;; 2 048 states, the control's answer.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; No candidate, and R is {∅}, one state: the second rung is taken wherever
;;; an obligation rule is loaded, and with nothing owed it proposes nothing.

(import std.algebra :symbols (total-owed))
(relation at Color House :why "{?1} is at {?2}")
(relation near House House :why "{?1} is near {?2}")
(is-a Red Color) (is-a H1 House) (is-a H2 House)
(at Red H1)
(solve)
